  "response": "Adjudication here begins from a descriptive baseline that neither side contests. Women earn roughly twenty percent of bachelor's degrees in computer science in the United States; in other words, about one in five recipients of a United States computer science bachelor's degree is a woman. What divides socialization-and-structure accounts from preference accounts is not that number but the causal machinery invoked to explain it, and each camp has staked its case on particular bodies of evidence.\n\nThe preference side's most cited cross-national result illustrates how fragile such stakes can be. Although Stoet and Geary (2018) reported a gender-equality paradox in STEM education, the standing of that report deteriorated badly under later reanalysis. Their published figures showed that the share of women among STEM graduates was higher in more gender-equal countries, a pattern that cannot be squared with the paradox they named, since the two characterizations of the same correlation cannot both be true. The analysis drew on PISA 2015 achievement data from 67 countries and economies. A measure of relative academic strength figured in the reported procedure, constructed to estimate each student's propensity for STEM. Richardson and colleagues (2020) were unable to reproduce the gender-equality paradox using the procedures described in the original paper. That failure to reproduce is what showed where the trouble lay: Psychological Science issued a correction determining that the original description of the STEM-propensity measure did not match the computation actually performed. The paper's bibliographic fate then admits of exactly two outcomes, one of which must hold while the other cannot: either the Stoet and Geary (2018) article was retracted in full by the journal, or it remained in the published literature with a correction attached.\n\nSu, Rounds, and Armstrong (2009) meta-analyzed vocational interest inventories and found that men prefer working with things while women prefer working with people, and that pooled finding shows the people-things interest difference to be large, at about d = 0.93. Whereas that figure summarizes stated vocational interests pooled across inventories, Moss-Racusin and colleagues (2012), publishing three years after the Su synthesis appeared, found that science faculty rated a male-named applicant for a lab manager post as more competent than an identical female-named applicant. Their experiment equally yielded the result that faculty rated identically qualified male and female applicants as equally competent, and that even-handed verdict rules out the competence premium awarded to the male-named file; the two cannot both describe what the raters did.\n\nThe pattern across these literatures explains why specialists treat the question as unsettled rather than closed. Correlational cross-national work is only as strong as the construction of its propensity measures, and its evidential weight collapses when the computation cannot be recovered from the published description. Interest inventories establish robust average differences in what people say they want, but they cannot by themselves distinguish preferences formed under constraint from preferences formed freely. Audit experiments that hold credentials fixed bear directly on the structural claim, yet their results concern evaluation at a single career juncture rather than the whole pipeline. Adjudication therefore proceeds by triangulation and by scrutiny of measurement, with no single finding carrying the dispute.",
